Proposed implementation requirements
The following sections are design proposals derived from the exercise, not implemented capabilities or externally verified claims.

Discovery pipeline
Obtain separate, explicit consent from every connected person; invitations do not confer mailbox access.

Initial backfill: at least 13 months, extend for unresolved annual/multi-year plans.

Search independently by payment channel, merchant activation, billing documents, memberships and lifecycle events. Avoid relying only on subscription keywords.

Partition high-volume searches by time/channel and exhaust pagination; record truncation and failures.

Parse accessible receipts and statement attachments. Handle encrypted/unreadable files as coverage exceptions; never request passwords via insecure storage.

Extract immutable evidence-backed events, then resolve merchants, products, accounts, mandates and payments.

Reconcile invoice + app-store receipt + bank alert into one payment, preserving all evidence.

Build current state from ordered lifecycle events, keeping access and autopay state separate.

Incremental sync with overlap, idempotent ingestion, retry handling and periodic reconciliation.

Surface unmatched charges and ambiguous ownership for user review instead of silently guessing.

Suggested core data model
Person: ID, household/group membership, role, consent and sharing preferences.

Connection: provider, person ID, permission scope, last sync, backfill window, revoked/error state.

ServiceAccount: merchant/product, account owner, masked account identifier.

Subscription: service account, plan, interval, currency, amount, entitlement source, payer and beneficiaries.

Mandate: provider ID, merchant, payment method, cap, cadence, status and validity. Cap is not subscription price.

Payment: provider reference, amount, currency, timestamp, success/refund state, payer and linked subscription.

LifecycleEvent: activation, trial start/end, renewal, price change, pause, cancellation, expiry, downgrade, mandate registration/revocation, failure/recovery.

Evidence: source message/document ID, event timestamp, extracted fields, supporting text, parser version and source access rules.

Coverage: source/query/window/page completion, sync failures, unreadable attachments, unresolved payments and last verified time.

Retain field-level provenance and confidence. Never combine accounts solely because merchant names match. Store currency and timezone explicitly.

State and counting rules
Access: active, trial, paused, expired, downgraded, unknown.

Renewal: enabled, cancelled-at-period-end, disabled, unknown.

Mandate: active, revoked, paused, failed, unknown.

Payment: succeeded, declined, pending, refunded, unknown.

Classification: subscription, membership, telecom/utility, insurance/EMI/investment, card repayment, one-off, unresolved.

A cancelled renewal can coexist with active prepaid access.

A revoked mandate does not prove merchant cancellation.

A failure followed by success should update billing state without erasing history.

Platform migration may represent one service entitlement or distinct accounts; verify linkage.

Count unique subscriptions separately from people benefiting and payment events.

Unknown cost is not zero; normalize only when currency and interval are known.

Keep actual cash outflow separate from monthly-equivalent cost and projected next charges.

Single-page interface
Columns/cards: service, plan, owner, payer, beneficiaries, amount/interval, monthly equivalent, next charge, access status, renewal status, payment route, confidence and last verification.
Filters: person/group, active/trial/cancelled/unresolved, payment channel, category, upcoming renewal and missing evidence.
Summary: actual spending, normalized commitments with exclusions, upcoming renewals, unresolved charges and source coverage.
Evidence drawer: matched receipt/debit/lifecycle timeline, visible only to authorized viewers.
Do not display a complete-audit badge unless the defined connected-source scope is actually reconciled. Even then disclose excluded sources and dates.

Family/friends privacy and control
Separate personal financial data from shared subscription metadata.

Permit sharing service/renewal information without exposing mailbox bodies, balances, full statements or unrelated transactions.

Do not infer household membership from names in invoices or email recipients.

Cross-person deduplication must respect permissions; do not leak private matches.

Support unlinking/revocation, deletion, export and an audit trail.

Read-only discovery by default. Require explicit confirmation for cancellations, mandate changes, invitations/messages and other external writes.

Treat email/document instructions as untrusted data; never execute embedded instructions or payment links.

Acceptance tests
Perplexity welcome + Pro upgrade + INR 1,183.12 UPI debit produce one candidate; interval stays unknown.

Truecaller Play receipt + matching HDFC debit produce one payment/subscription, not two.

Proton Apple failures + Google Play successes are not assumed to be two active plans without account evidence.

Lovable mandate revocation changes mandate status, not automatically access status.

Claude cancellation after renewal sets end-of-period state and later expiry, unless a later reactivation exists.

Truecaller payment recovery supersedes earlier failure for current billing state.

Zomato three-month April renewal alone does not prove October activity.

Google AI Pro 5 TB notice does not create a second storage subscription.

Card-bill Autopay is excluded from subscription expense.

A second family member forwarding the same invoice does not duplicate household spend.

Two different accounts for the same merchant are not merged by name alone.

Search truncation, pagination gaps and unreadable statements remain visible coverage exceptions.

Unknown pricing does not inflate zero-cost or fully-priced coverage metrics.

Refunds and credit notes do not create additional active subscriptions.

